; bfsodium SHA_512 HASHCORE : the hash  over a prefix in memory and then the wire
;
; HAND WRITTEN skeleton; The round and the schedule step are each PASTED from
; their own verified files; what is here is the constant table  the padding and
; the conveyors;
;
; INTERFACE entry=259 exit=1836 footprint=0:1859
; IO  in:  plen{2} LE  slen{2} LE  pbuf{256}  then slen bytes from the wire
;     out: digest{64}  big endian  as the standard prints it
;
; The message is plen bytes taken from pbuf  followed by slen bytes read from
; the wire; either count may be nought; Only the first plen bytes of pbuf are
; looked at  and the rest may hold anything;
;
; TWO SOURCES  because two callers want different ones; sha512 hashes what is
; on the wire and leaves plen at nought; HMAC hashes a block it has built in
; memory  a key exclusive ored with a pad  and then the message; HKDF hashes
; only what it has built  and leaves slen at nought; One padding loop and one
; block loop serve all three;
;
; TAPE MAP  (home @0)
;   @0x000:0x001  plen{2} u16 LE  prefix bytes not yet taken; SPENT
;   @0x002:0x003  slen{2} u16 LE  wire bytes not yet taken; SPENT
;   @0x004:0x103  pbuf{256} u8    the prefix; consumed from its head
;   @0x104:0x143  H{8}    u64 LE  the running hash  and then the digest
;   @0x144:0x183  v{8}    u64 LE  a b c d e f g h
;   @0x184:0x203  w{16}   u64 LE  the schedule window
;   @0x204:0x213  Wt and Wc  this round's schedule word and the window's copy
;   @0x214:0x219  pre rnd f16 nf tt uu   the round counters and their flags
;   @0x21c:0x223  tmp{8}  hands a word back after it has been copied
;   @0x228:0x243  the adder frame; add64 footprint 0:27 at @0x228
;   @0x248:0x35c  sha512/round's frame; its footprint is 0:276 pasted there
;   @0x360:0x424  sha512/expand's frame; its footprint is 0:196
;   @0x428:0x6a7  K{80}   u64 LE  the constants; the table ROTATES one word per
;                          round  so K{t} is always at its head and after eighty
;                          rounds it is back where it started  which is what
;                          lets a second block use it again
;   @0x6a8:0x727  blk{128} u8     the block being built
;   @0x72a:0x732  the padding's flags  its byte counter and the cells the two
;                          counts are tested through
;   @0x734:0x73b  len{8}  u64 LE  the message length  kept for the last block
;   @0x73c:0x743  ksave{8}        holds K{t} while the table rotates past it
;
; THE SAME SHAPE AS sha256/hashcore  section for section  and the two should be
; read together; What changes is the width of a word  the size of a block  the
; number of rounds  the constants  and the sixteen byte length field instead of
; eight; THE FLAGS  THE LENGTH AND KSAVE SIT AT THE SAME OFFSETS ABOVE THE TOP
; OF THE BLOCK AS THEY DO THERE  which is on purpose: it means every distance
; in the padding machinery measured from the block top is the same number in
; both files  and only the ones reaching down into plen  slen  pbuf and the
; frames had to be worked out again;
;
; The tables are CLEARED before they are written  because writing a byte here
; ADDS to what the cell held; HMAC enters this routine three times over and the
; second entry would otherwise find the first digest still in H and add the
; initial words to it; The constants do survive a call untouched  since the
; table rotates a full eighty places per block and comes back to where it
; started  but they are cleared too: a routine that is safe to enter twice
; should not rest on that being noticed;
;
; The prefix buffer is at the BOTTOM  with the two counts  because a read
; prologue may hold only reads and single steps: a long run of steps before the
; first read is split across lines by the layout pass  and a paste begins at the
; prologue  so those steps would be silently dropped; tools/bffoot refuses a
; routine shaped that way;
;
; FOUR CONVEYORS AND NO INDEX; the schedule window slides so its four taps are
; always in the same cells  the constants rotate so the round always reads the
; head of the table  the block slides so a byte always lands on its top  and
; the prefix is consumed from its head and slides after each byte;
;
; A NOTE ON THE LENGTH; SHA_512 ends a message with a HUNDRED AND TWENTY EIGHT
; bit length where SHA_256 uses sixty four; Only the low eight bytes are ever
; written  because the byte count is kept in four cells and a message of more
; than four gigabytes is not something a brainfuck program is going to be
; handed; the other eight bytes of the field are the nought the block was
; cleared to;

; read the two counts and the prefix buffer
,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,
; ASSERT ptr=259

; ==== the eighty round constants ====
; A table because the rounds are a loop; each line is one constant  eight
; bytes little endian  each byte the shorter of counting up from nought or
; down through the wrap;
; the table is emptied first  so a second entry is safe
R805
